  "author": "raj-tomar",
  "body": "Aldar Properties has completed Abu Dhabi's first registration of a mortgage on an off-plan property with Abu Dhabi Commercial Bank, under a framework launched by the Abu Dhabi Real Estate Centre, according to ADREC. The framework allows buyers who have paid 50 per cent of an eligible off-plan unit's value to mortgage the outstanding amount before construction is complete, ADREC stated.\n\nUnder the system, the financing bank can be recorded on the mortgage registration certificate before handover, ADREC said. Banks can fund remaining instalments during construction and the final payment due at handover, according to the regulator. ADREC said mortgage interests in eligible units can be entered in the Initial Real Estate Register, creating a formal record for buyers, developers and lenders.\n\nGhazi Saeed Alateibi, executive director of the real estate transaction sector at ADREC, said the completion of the registration demonstrated ADREC's off-plan mortgage registration service in live market use. By enabling mortgage interests in eligible off-plan real estate units to be recorded in the Initial Real Estate Register, the service strengthens transparency and provides greater clarity and protection for buyers, developers and financial institutions, Alateibi stated. The service is available across the market to participating institutions that meet the relevant requirements, ADREC confirmed.\n\nThe 50 per cent payment threshold is aligned with UAE Central Bank regulations, according to Aldar. The Central Bank requires customers to have paid 50 per cent of the property price to be eligible for off-plan mortgage financing, Aldar stated. Under the new system, a customer who has paid 50 per cent of the purchase price can arrange a mortgage against their off-plan property, with the bank funding the remaining instalments and the final handover payment, according to ADREC.\n\nThe framework addresses a structural constraint that has historically required off-plan buyers to self-fund construction-stage instalments in full. By enabling mortgage drawdowns during the build period, the mechanism preserves buyer liquidity and locks in financing terms at the point of mortgage approval rather than at handover. The move could give buyers earlier certainty over borrowing costs and reduce the need to tie up cash while a property is being built, according to ADREC.\n\nThe framework also gives lenders a formal interest in the property before completion, potentially strengthening protections in the event of a dispute, ADREC stated. That pre-handover mortgage registration creates a legal charge on the unit while it remains in the Initial Real Estate Register, the precinct-level ledger ADREC maintains for projects under construction. Upon completion, the unit transfers to the emirate's main real estate register.\n\nFaisal Falaknaz, Aldar's chief financial and sustainability officer, said the new off-plan mortgage framework introduced by ADREC marks an important step in further enhancing the transparency and accessibility of the market. Aldar is proud to have worked with ADREC and ADCB to complete the first transaction, translating the framework into greater financing flexibility for homebuyers, Falaknaz stated.\n\nAldar completed the first registration under the new framework, with Abu Dhabi Commercial Bank supporting the initial transactions as the mortgage bank, according to Aldar. Off-plan financing, including payments during construction, is now available to Aldar customers who have paid 50 per cent or more of the property value at eligible projects, Aldar confirmed.\n\nAldar is offering the financing route through Home Finance by Aldar, its in-house mortgage advisory service, according to the developer. The service is free to customers and provides access to mortgage options from more than six conventional and Islamic banks, Aldar stated. The mortgages are being facilitated without fees through Home Finance by Aldar, an in-house advisory service that connects customers to more than six participating conventional and Islamic banks, according to Aldar.\n\nParticipating lenders listed by Aldar include ADCB, Abu Dhabi Islamic Bank, Dubai Islamic Bank, Emirates NBD, Emirates Islamic and First Abu Dhabi Bank. Customers can access the service through the Live Aldar app and request a call back from the Home Finance by Aldar team, Aldar stated.\n\nThe first registration comes as Abu Dhabi's off-plan market expands, with developers increasingly relying on pre-completion sales to fund construction, according to market observers. The new framework could allow eligible buyers to secure financing earlier, while giving banks a clearer legal position before properties are handed over, ADREC stated.\n\nThe framework represents a structural shift that permits the financing bank to be recorded on the mortgage registration certificate before handover, departing from the traditional model in which buyers arrange mortgages only at completion. This gives buyers certainty over financing terms in advance, preserves liquidity during construction, and removes the need to arrange a mortgage at completion, offering an alternative to traditional handover-stage financing with wider choice over rates and terms, according to ADREC.\n\nThe Initial Real Estate Register, into which these mortgages are now recorded, functions as a transitional ledger for units that have been sold but not yet completed. It provides a legal basis for ownership and encumbrance claims during the construction period, bridging the gap between contract signature and title deed issuance. The register's existence has been a feature of Abu Dhabi's off-plan regulatory architecture, but the new framework formalises its use for mortgage registration.\n\nThe framework is available on a market-wide basis to participating institutions that meet the relevant requirements, though ADREC has not disclosed the specific eligibility criteria. ADCB's role as the inaugural lender suggests that participating banks must demonstrate operational readiness to integrate with ADREC's digital mortgage registration platform and meet any capital or compliance thresholds the regulator has set for off-plan lending.",
